ANONYMOUS MULTIPART PARSE ON THE PRIVATE WORKSPACE UPLOAD ROUTES
Audit host, 2026-09-04. Artifact BUILD_ID 1nfPtJYCTsqaZBbSstXKc, built at 388e8af.
Origin https://172.20.10.2:3051 (TLS front) -> 127.0.0.1:3052.

Why this file exists. Checking that the R13 malformed-body fix had no unguarded
sibling meant listing every `request.formData()` call site in shipped code. All five
are guarded. Two other things showed up while looking, and both were then MEASURED
rather than read.

── the five call sites, all guarded ───────────────────────────────────
$ grep -rn '\.formData()' app/ lib/ src/ components/ --include='*.ts' --include='*.tsx' | grep -v '\.test\.'
  app/api/workspaces/[workspaceId]/documents/[documentId]/versions/upload/route.ts:119
  app/api/workspaces/[workspaceId]/documents/[documentId]/attachments/route.ts:80
  app/api/workspaces/[workspaceId]/documents/upload/route.ts:83
  lib/server/toolJobSubmit.ts:131          <- guarded at 388e8af
  lib/server/processingJobSubmit.ts:92     <- guarded at 388e8af
The three Workspace routes already answered 400/422 for an unparseable body before
this audit; that is the guard the 388e8af fix copied onto the tool paths.

── finding 1: the same input, two different answers ───────────────────
One hand-built body, filename carrying a raw `"`, posted to two routes:

  attachments      -> HTTP 422  {"code":"INVALID_INPUT","message":"A multipart upload is required."}
  documents/upload -> HTTP 400  {"code":"INVALID_INPUT","message":"Malformed multipart body."}

422 is the wrong code for a body that cannot be parsed, and the message misdescribes
the cause: a multipart upload WAS supplied, it just could not be read. Cosmetic, and
recorded as P3 rather than changed, because changing shipped code here would
invalidate the frozen artifact every gate in §30 was measured against.

── finding 2: the parse happens before authentication ─────────────────
On all three Workspace upload routes the only gate ahead of `request.formData()` is
`requireSameOrigin`. `workspaceId` and `documentId` come from the URL path, so
authorization COULD precede the parse. Measured with no session cookie at all:

$ POST /api/workspaces/ws_probe/documents/doc_probe/attachments   (Origin set, NO cookie)
  well-formed 13-byte body              HTTP 422  {"code":"INVALID_INPUT","message":"Invalid attachment fields."}
  well-formed 8 MiB body                HTTP 422  {"code":"INVALID_INPUT","message":"Invalid attachment fields."}

"Invalid attachment fields" is FIELD validation, which is downstream of the parse —
so an unauthenticated caller had 8 MiB buffered and parsed on its behalf.

── the bound is real, and it is cheap ─────────────────────────────────
$ POST same route, declared AND sent 26.0 MiB, anonymous
  HTTP 413 in 0.03s  {"code":"PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE","message":"This attachment is too large."}

0.03s means the declared content-length is refused before the body is read, not after.
The ceiling is L.maxAttachmentBytes + 64 KiB, and maxAttachmentBytes is 25 MiB
(src/domain/entities/DocumentMetadata.ts:55). So the work an anonymous caller can
demand is bounded per request at ~25 MiB.

── what is NOT bounded: the number of requests ────────────────────────
$ for f in $(grep -rln 'rateLimit' app/api/ | grep -v '\.test\.'); do ...; done
  app/api/tools/[slug]/route.ts        RateLimiter   <- the PUBLIC tool route
  app/api/jobs/route.ts                RateLimiter
  app/api/admin/setup/route.ts         RateLimiter
  app/api/admin/login/route.ts         RateLimiter
  app/api/csp-report/route.ts          RateLimiter
  app/api/analytics/events/route.ts    RateLimiter
  app/api/billing/checkout/route.ts    clientIp only, no limiter
  app/api/billing/portal/route.ts      clientIp only, no limiter
$ grep -rln 'rateLimit' app/api/workspaces/
  (none)
$ grep -n 'rateLimit\|rate-limit' proxy.ts
  (none — there is no limiter at the middleware layer either)
Six shipped routes construct a limiter. None of the three Workspace upload routes
does, and nothing upstream of them does. The PUBLIC tool route is limited; these
PRIVATE ones are not — which is the inversion worth writing down.

── classification ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
P2, open, not a launch blocker and not inflated into one:
  · no access control is bypassed — authorization still precedes every read and
    every write, and the documented "AUTHORIZATION BEFORE STORAGE" invariant in
    versions/upload/route.ts:81 holds;
  · nothing is disclosed — the anonymous caller learns only that its fields are
    invalid, the same answer for a workspace that does not exist;
  · R16 is unaffected: the ceilings this audit was asked to check are enforced, and
    this run is additional live evidence that one of them is.
What it is: bounded parse work per request, unbounded in request count, on endpoints
that are meant to be private. The mitigation is a rate-limit policy, which is an
infrastructure decision this audit is not authorized to make -> §37 LAUNCH DECISION
REQUIRED.

No cookie, token or session value appears above; the probe sent none.
